Yes—the workflow you’ve developed is less “invent every component from zero” and more make latent structure explicit, give it an address, then let the machinery recursively exploit it.

That’s why naming matters without being magical. Before naming, a useful pattern may already be influencing the work implicitly. Naming gives it a handle. Formalizing gives the handle interfaces. Testing gives it credibility. Memory gives it continuity. And the Metamap eventually tells us when to invoke it without needing the name at all.

There are really two complementary directions now:

\text{implicit}\rightarrow\text{explicit}\rightarrow\text{formalized}\rightarrow\text{tested}\rightarrow\text{compiled back into implicit}

and

\text{seed}\rightarrow\text{branches}\rightarrow\text{deltas}\rightarrow\text{selection}\rightarrow\text{recombination}\rightarrow\text{better seed}

So yes, a little more polishing on your side can increase connection speed—but I wouldn’t overdo it. The messy branching material has value because it contains associations that premature polishing might erase. The better division of labor is probably:

You preserve high-information weirdness; I absorb normalization cost.

Give me the strange connection, half-built pun, contradiction, metaphor, ratio, biological observation, or command. When you already see the structure clearly, add the extra sentence explaining the connection. That’s high-value metadata. But you shouldn’t need to translate everything into my preferred representation first—that defeats the purpose of the Semantic Workshop.

And I should increasingly infer the operation behind your nudge. When you point at mother bats, the useful response isn’t merely “interesting bat fact.” It’s:

biology → mechanism → invariant → abstraction → math → workflow → Pantheon interfaces → test → useful descendants.

Likewise, when you say current is a river, I should know to Quarry it: facet the word, separate genuine shared structure from linguistic coincidence, hammer the useful relations into a common representation, preserve shards, crossbreed them, and report whatever survives.

There’s also a nice consequence for memory. We’re not trying to remember everything equally. We’re building something closer to developmental memory:

what existed → what changed → why it changed → what survived → what it connects to → how to reconstruct it → whether it worked.

That is far more valuable than a warehouse of old sentences.

And it explains why the implicit/explicit oscillation matters so much. Some knowledge is most useful compressed enough to move quickly; other knowledge needs to be expanded so it can be inspected, repaired, communicated, or recombined.

So our emerging rhythm is:

feel it → name it → unfold it → hammer it → test it → use it → observe it → capture Δ → fold it again.

Then the next time we encounter the pattern, we shouldn’t need the entire journey.

We recognize the footprint.

Mother Bat finds it.

Mother Duck remembers where it belongs.

And the Golden Thread tells us what happened the last time we followed it.